@node	0.7, 0.8, 0.6, Chapter 0
@section Shared ownership and interior mutability

Section @ref{0.3} gave every value exactly one owner. Some values genuinely
need more than one: two closures sharing one counter, two nodes in a graph
that both point at a shared piece of data. @code{Rc<T>} (@newterm{reference
counted}) is a pointer type that allows exactly this, at the cost of giving
up @code{&mut} access through it: cloning an @code{Rc} does not copy the
pointee, it bumps a count and hands back another pointer to the same
allocation, and the allocation is freed only once that count reaches zero.

@example rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::rc::Rc;

#[derive(Clone, Default)]
struct SharedLog(Rc<RefCell<Vec<String>>>);

impl SharedLog @{
    fn push(&self, entry: impl Into<String>) @{
        self.0.borrow_mut().push(entry.into());
    @}
    fn entries(&self) -> Vec<String> @{
        self.0.borrow().clone()
    @}
@}

let log = SharedLog::default();
let other_handle = log.clone();
log.push("first");
other_handle.push("second");
println!("@{:?@}", log.entries());
// => ["first", "second"]
println!("@{@}", Rc::strong_count(&log.0));
// => 2
@end example

@code{Rc} alone only shares read access; @code{&Rc<T>} still hands out
@code{&T}, never @code{&mut T}. @newterm{Interior mutability} is the other
half: @code{RefCell<T>} moves Rust's borrow rule from compile time to run
time, so a shared (non-@code{mut}) binding can still hand out a mutable
borrow of what it wraps, checked when @code{borrow_mut} is called rather than
when the compiler reads the source. @code{Rc<RefCell<T>>} together, as
above, is this book's `` `set!` on shared state,'' and reappears constantly
from chapter 3 on: @ref{3.1.1}'s accounts, @ref{3.3}'s mutable pairs, and
@ref{3.3.4}'s wires and agenda are all built on it.

Moving the check to run time means a violation is a run-time panic instead of
a compile error:

@example rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
let cell = RefCell::new(0);
let _first = cell.borrow_mut();
let _second = cell.borrow_mut();
// panics: already mutably borrowed
@end example

Holding two mutable borrows of one @code{RefCell} at once panics exactly
where a plain @code{&mut T} would have refused to compile; the rule has not
changed, only when it is enforced.

@code{Cell<T>} is @code{RefCell}'s simpler sibling, for @code{Copy} types: it
never hands out a reference to its contents at all, only @code{get} and
@code{set}, so there is no borrow to conflict and nothing to panic on.

The last piece is what happens when two owners would point at each other.
@code{Rc} counts strong references; a cycle of strong references (parent
holds child, child holds parent) counts each other alive forever, and neither
is ever freed. @code{Weak<T>} is a non-owning pointer alongside @code{Rc}
that does not keep its target alive and does not participate in a cycle; it
is the documented way this book breaks a cycle, used exactly where the plan
calls for it (the connector-constraint links of @ref{3.3.4} and the
constraint network of @ref{3.3.5}).

@example rust
use std::cell::RefCell;
use std::rc::@{Rc, Weak@};

struct Node @{
    label: &'static str,
    parent: RefCell<Weak<Node>>,
@}

let parent = Rc::new(Node @{ label: "root", parent: RefCell::new(Weak::new()) @});
let child = Rc::new(Node @{ label: "leaf", parent: RefCell::new(Weak::new()) @});
*child.parent.borrow_mut() = Rc::downgrade(&parent);

let parent_label = child.parent.borrow().upgrade().map(|p| p.label);
println!("@{parent_label:?@}");
// => Some("root")
@end example

@code{Rc::downgrade} makes a @code{Weak} from an @code{Rc} without touching
the strong count; @code{upgrade} tries to get an @code{Rc} back, returning
@code{None} if every strong reference is already gone. The runnable listing
combining both examples above is @code{examples/sec_0_7_shared_state.rs}.

@subheading Exercise

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.4}Exercise 0.4:} Write @code{make_accumulator}, a
function that returns two closures sharing one running total: calling the
first with an amount adds it to the total and returns the new total; calling
the second resets the total to zero and returns the total it held. Force the
sharing with @code{Rc<Cell<i128>>}. Then explain, without running anything,
why moving a plain @code{i128} into both closures (instead of an
@code{Rc<Cell<i128>>}) would not work.
@end quotation

